---
import { RETENTION_DAYS } from "@trilleo/storage";
import InfoLayout from "../../layouts/InfoLayout.astro";
import { NEW_DEVICE_DAYS, SECURITY_LOG_DAYS } from "../../lib/auth/activity";
import { UNDO_EMAIL_DAYS } from "../../lib/auth/email-change";
import { SIGN_IN_LIMITS } from "../../lib/auth/email-sign-in";
import { SESSION_TTL_MS } from "../../lib/auth/sessions";
import { USERNAME_HOLD_DAYS } from "../../lib/auth/usernames";
import { CONTACT_RETENTION_DAYS } from "../../lib/contact/store";
import { CODE_LIMITS, CODE_RETENTION_DAYS } from "../../lib/mail/addresses";
import { MAIL_LOG_DAYS, MAIL_RETENTION_DAYS } from "../../lib/mail/outbox";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAME } from "../../lib/site";

const sessionDays = SESSION_TTL_MS / (24 * 60 * 60 * 1000);
// deploy/server/trilleo-backup keeps this many days of nightly dumps (KEEP_DAYS).
const BACKUP_DAYS = 14;
const codeMinutes = CODE_LIMITS.ttlMs / 60_000;
const signInMinutes = SIGN_IN_LIMITS.ttlMs / 60_000;

const sections = [
  { slug: "summary", text: "In short" },
  { slug: "who", text: "Who is responsible" },
  { slug: "what", text: "What is kept, and why" },
  { slug: "not-kept", text: "What isn’t collected" },
  { slug: "contact-messages", text: "Contact messages" },
  { slug: "email", text: "Email" },
  { slug: "sharing", text: "Who else handles it" },
  { slug: "where", text: "Where it is stored" },
  { slug: "retention", text: "How long it is kept" },
  { slug: "rights", text: "Your rights" },
  { slug: "children", text: "Children" },
  { slug: "security", text: "Keeping it safe" },
] as const;
---

<InfoLayout path="/legal/privacy/" sections={sections} history>
  <h2 id="summary">In short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      You can read, use the tools and play without an account. Then nothing is
      kept about you on the server: tool data and game saves stay in your
      browser.
    </li>
    <li>
      An account is its email address: you sign in with it (a code, no
      password), or with a GitHub account you link to it. The site keeps your
      address, the username you choose, what you write or upload, and when you
      signed in.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your address is only used to sign you in, for the notifications you choose
      and the codes you ask for, never for newsletters, and every notification
      has an unsubscribe link.
    </li>
    <li>
      There are no ads and no tracking. Page views are counted by Cloudflare Web
      Analytics, which sets no cookies and doesn’t identify you. Fonts are
      served from this site.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can download everything kept about you, or delete your account, from
      your account page at any time.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="who">Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE_NAME} (www.trilleo.net) is run by an individual known as Trilleo,
    based in mainland China, who decides what is collected and why: the
    “personal information handler” under China’s Personal Information Protection
    Law (PIPL), and the “controller” in the sense of the EU and UK GDPR. In this
    policy, “I” means Trilleo. Write to{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or use the{" "}
    <a href="/contact/">contact form</a> about anything here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what">What is kept, and why</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">What</th>
        <th scope="col">When</th>
        <th scope="col">Why</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Your email address and the username you choose (and, for{" "}
          {USERNAME_HOLD_DAYS} days after you change it, the old one, so links
          to it still work)
        </td>
        <td>You make an account</td>
        <td>To know it’s you, sign you in, and sign your comments</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          A linked GitHub account: its user ID, username and public name
          (refreshed when you use it), and when it was linked and last used
        </td>
        <td>You sign in with GitHub, or link it</td>
        <td>To let you sign in with it</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Your profile: display name, pronouns, location, a status line, a bio,
          links, and your privacy choices
        </td>
        <td>You fill it in (all optional)</td>
        <td>To show your profile the way you chose</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Passkeys: each one’s public key (its private half never leaves your
          device or password manager), a name, and when it was added and last
          used
        </td>
        <td>You add a passkey</td>
        <td>To let you sign in with it</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          A security log: sign-ins and how, address changes, passkeys and linked
          accounts added or removed, and sign-outs, each with a rough browser
          name (such as “Firefox on Windows”) and the time
        </td>
        <td>Those things happen to your account</td>
        <td>
          To show you what happened, and to warn you about a sign-in from a
          browser your account hasn’t used in {NEW_DEVICE_DAYS} days
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Sessions: a fingerprint (SHA-256) of your sign-in cookie, your
          browser’s User-Agent, and when it was created and last used
        </td>
        <td>Each signed-in browser</td>
        <td>To keep you signed in, and to let you see and end your sessions</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Comments, and their status (waiting, published, hidden)</td>
        <td>You comment</td>
        <td>To publish them, and to moderate</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Tool data (such as notes) and game saves, Bazaar orders and trades
        </td>
        <td>You use a tool or play while signed in</td>
        <td>
          To keep them in your account. Trades are recorded without names, for
          price history
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Uploaded files, their names, sizes, types and checksums, reports you
          make or receive, appeals, and moderation decisions
        </td>
        <td>You upload, report or appeal</td>
        <td>To store and serve files, and to keep the site safe and legal</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Minecraft projects: their pages (name, description, tags, links,
          licence), pictures, releases and their files, and reports you make
          about projects
        </td>
        <td>You share a creation, or report one</td>
        <td>
          To publish it, credited to you (your display name if your profile is
          public, otherwise your username), and to moderate
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Download counts: one number per file per day</td>
        <td>Someone downloads a file</td>
        <td>
          To show the owner how often it’s downloaded. Who downloaded it is not
          recorded
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Which notifications you want, and a record of the email sent to you
          (address, subject, kind, when, and whether it went; the text itself
          for a limited time)
        </td>
        <td>You have an account</td>
        <td>
          To send the notifications you chose and the codes you ask for (see
          below)
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Messages from the contact form</td>
        <td>You write one</td>
        <td>To read and answer it (see below)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The basis for all of this is that you asked for it: signing in, writing,
    uploading or saving is something you do to use a feature (PIPL article
    13(2), GDPR article 6(1)(b)). Moderation records, and keeping files someone
    reported, rest on legal duties and the legitimate interest in a safe site
    (PIPL article 13(3), GDPR article 6(1)(c) and (f)).
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-kept">What isn’t collected</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      No advertising or social-media trackers, and no fingerprinting. The one
      outside script is Cloudflare Web Analytics: it counts page views without
      cookies or identifiers, and I see only totals (pages, referring sites,
      countries, browsers), never individual visitors.
    </li>
    <li>
      Nothing else from GitHub: sign-in asks only to read your public profile
      and your addresses. If GitHub has verified your primary address, a new
      account uses it (an account from before email sign-in takes it, if no
      other account has it); otherwise you’re asked for one. GitHub’s access
      token is used once and thrown away. An address alone never links a GitHub
      account to an account here.
    </li>
    <li>
      No IP address is stored. To count a download once per visitor per day, a
      hash of your address and browser is held in the server’s memory for that
      day only; the contact form keeps a similar hash in memory for a day to
      limit spam.
    </li>
    <li>
      Emails carry no tracking pixels or tracked links: nobody learns whether
      you opened one.
    </li>
    <li>
      Files are scanned for malware on the server, but nothing in them is used
      for anything else. The file tools (convert, file info, QR, colour) work
      entirely in your browser: what you open in them is never uploaded.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="contact-messages">Contact messages</h2>
  <p>
    The contact form asks for a name (any name will do), an optional email
    address for the reply, and your message. If you are signed in, the message
    is also linked to your account and appears in your data export. Messages are
    read only by me, are never used for marketing, and are deleted{" "}
    {CONTACT_RETENTION_DAYS} days after they arrive at the latest, or sooner if
    you ask. If you left an address, I may answer by email; the answer is kept
    like any other email (below).
  </p>

  <h2 id="email">Email</h2>
  <p>
    To sign in by email, the site sends your address a code that works once, for{" "}
    {signInMinutes} minutes. To change your address (on your{" "}
    <a href="/account/email/">account’s email page</a>), it sends the new one a
    code that works for {codeMinutes} minutes. Only a fingerprint (SHA-256) of
    each code is stored, and it is deleted after {CODE_RETENTION_DAYS} days. An
    address belongs to one account. To limit abuse, a hash of your IP address is
    held in the server’s memory for a day to count the codes asked for.
  </p>
  <p>
    With an address, you get the notifications you leave switched on: replies to
    your comments, your comments’ review, and decisions about your uploads and
    Minecraft projects, and sign-in alerts. Each email has a one-click
    unsubscribe link, and the email page lets you switch each kind off. Since
    it’s how you sign in, the address can be changed but not removed, except by
    deleting your account. Codes you ask for always arrive, and so does a
    message to your old address when it changes, with a link that undoes the
    change for {UNDO_EMAIL_DAYS} days.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site keeps a record of each email it sends: to whom, the subject, what
    kind it was, when, and whether it was delivered. The text is deleted{" "}
    {MAIL_RETENTION_DAYS} days after sending (a code’s text as soon as it has
    gone), and the rest of the record after {MAIL_LOG_DAYS} days. It appears in
    your data export.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sharing">Who else handles it</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing is sold or shared for advertising. A few services handle data on the
    site’s behalf, only to run it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Huawei Cloud</strong> hosts the server, the database and uploaded
      files, in mainland China.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong> sits in front of the site and handles every
      request (including your IP address) to deliver it and protect it from
      attacks, and counts page views (Web Analytics, described above). See
      Cloudflare’s own privacy policy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tencent Cloud</strong> sends the site’s email (its Simple Email
      Service, in Hong Kong). It receives each message: the address it goes to
      and what it says.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GitHub</strong> handles signing in with GitHub, if you use it.
      Then GitHub learns that you used this site.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Public things are public: published comments, public profiles, published
    files, Minecraft projects and leaderboard entries can be seen by anyone, and
    may be copied by search engines. Information may also be disclosed when the
    law requires it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where">Where it is stored</h2>
  <p>
    The server, database and files are in mainland China. If you use the site
    from elsewhere, what you send is transferred there; Cloudflare may process
    requests in the data centre nearest to you. Email goes out through Tencent
    Cloud in Hong Kong. By signing in or sending a message you agree to this
    transfer, and you can withdraw that by deleting your account.
  </p>

  <h2 id="retention">How long it is kept</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your account, profile, comments, tool data and saves: until you delete
      them or your account.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sessions: until you sign out, or {sessionDays} days after they were last
      renewed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Deleted files: {RETENTION_DAYS.deleted} days (so a mistake can be undone),
      then the file itself is erased. Files taken down by moderation:{" "}
      {RETENTION_DAYS.removed} days, as evidence and to allow an appeal. A
      record that the file existed (its name, checksum and status) stays, to
      stop the same file being uploaded again.
    </li>
    <li>Contact messages: at most {CONTACT_RETENTION_DAYS} days.</li>
    <li>
      The security log: {SECURITY_LOG_DAYS} days. Passkeys: until you remove
      them or delete your account.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your email address and username: until you change them or delete your
      account (an old username: {USERNAME_HOLD_DAYS} days). The text of emails
      sent to you: {MAIL_RETENTION_DAYS} days (codes: until sent); the record
      that an email was sent: {MAIL_LOG_DAYS} days. Codes: {CODE_RETENTION_DAYS}{" "}
      days.
    </li>
    <li>
      Backups of the database are kept for {BACKUP_DAYS} days, so deleted data
      disappears from them within that time.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
  <p>Wherever you live, you can:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>See and copy</strong> what is kept:{" "}
      <a href="/account/">your account</a> has a “Download your data” link (a
      JSON file with everything).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Correct</strong> it: edit your{" "}
      <a href="/account/profile/">profile</a> and username, or your address on
      your <a href="/account/email/">email page</a>; a linked GitHub account’s
      name updates when you next use it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Delete</strong> it: delete comments or files one by one, sign
      other browsers out, or delete your whole account from your account page.
      That removes your profile, comments, tool data, saves, files, messages,
      your email address, linked accounts and the record of email sent to you.
      You can also unlink GitHub on your{" "}
      <a href="/account/security/">security page</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Withdraw consent, object, or restrict</strong> a use (for email:
      unsubscribe, or switch notifications off), and ask how a decision about
      you was made. Nothing here is decided about you automatically, except spam
      limits and malware checks, which a person can review.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Complain</strong> to a regulator: in China, the Cyberspace
      Administration (CAC); in the EU or UK, your data protection authority.
      Please write to me first; I would like the chance to fix it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For anything you can’t do yourself, <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a>. I
    answer within 15 days, and may ask you to show it’s your account (for
    example, by signing in).
  </p>

  <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
  <p>
    You need to be at least 14 to make an account. Younger visitors are welcome
    to read, use the tools and play without signing in. If you think a child
    under 14 has an account, tell me and it will be deleted.
  </p>

  <h2 id="security">Keeping it safe</h2>
  <p>
    Everything travels over HTTPS. There are no passwords to leak: sign-in is by
    a one-time code or GitHub. The server keeps only a hash of your sign-in
    token, and the cookie that carries it is secure, HTTP-only and limited to
    this site. Uploaded files are kept in a private bucket unless they are
    published, and are checked for malware. Backups stay on the server. No
    system is perfect: if something goes wrong that affects your data, you will
    be told, and so will the authorities where the law requires it. To report a
    weakness, see the <a href="/security/">security policy</a>.
  </p>
</InfoLayout>
